### **Cyber Law, Compliance & Digital Forensics**
* **Cyberspace & Regulation**: Cyberspace is a virtual environment of interconnected networks [7]. Cyber security regulations establish legal rules (e.g., IT Act 2000 covering electronic transactions and cybercrimes) [7].
* **International Law Challenges**: Difficult to enforce because cyberspace transcends national borders and attack attribution is hard [7].
* **Digital Forensics Lifecycle**: Systematic 6-phase process: **Identification** \\(\rightarrow\\) **Preservation** (securing scene, preventing tampering) \\(\rightarrow\\) **Collection** (creating bit-by-bit images of storage devices) \\(\rightarrow\\) **Examination** (recovering deleted data, decrypting files) \\(\rightarrow\\) **Analysis** (correlating findings, reconstructing events) \\(\rightarrow\\) **Presentation** (writing forensic reports for court) [8, 9].
* **Digital Evidence & Signatures**: Digital evidence is binary-stored data on disks or emails [9]. Digital signatures verify authenticity, integrity, and origin [9]. Forensics is critical because digital evidence is fragile and courts require admissible evidence [8].

> **AWS Fresher Interview Mapping**: Forensic collection in AWS involves taking **EBS Volume Snapshots** (bit-by-bit copies) of compromised EC2 instances, isolating instances using restricted Security Groups, and auditing event timelines using **AWS CloudTrail** and **VPC Flow Logs**.

